fact WorkItem(id: "WI-891", description: some(value: "cpp-gen's UNLOWERABLE-BODY DEGRADE EMITS `return {};`, which COMPILES and answers a zero-initialized value — so a diagnosed gap becomes a silently wrong PROGRAM, and the diagnosis is a comment nobody has to read.\n\nWHERE. `synthesise_body_for` (rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs): when `lower_node` fails on an operation's expression body, it returns\n\n    // TODO: cannot lower expression body — <message>\n    return {};\n\nThe comment's stated intent is right — 'the user sees \"this op has a body but cpp-gen can't lower it yet\" immediately at codegen time, not as a compile error about a missing body later' — and for a CAPABILITY GAP in the generator that is the correct shape: the fix is in cpp-gen, the author can see which method is affected, and the rest of the header still emits. What is wrong is the PAYLOAD. `return {};` is valid C++ for every type that is value-initializable, so `int64_t` becomes 0, `bool` becomes false, `double` becomes 0.0, `std::string` becomes empty. The generated project BUILDS, and the operation answers a plausible constant.\n\nMEASURED during WI-886: an unmapped `Int64.compare` produced exactly this, and it was strictly LESS loud than the defect WI-886 was removing — the bogus `compare(a, b)` it replaced at least failed the C++ build. WI-886 handled its own class by refusing outright (`CodegenContext::unrealized_refusal`, and `synthesise_body_for` re-raises rather than degrading), which leaves the degrade in place for the two classes it was built for: a self-referential anonymous lambda (`unsupported_test::recursive_anonymous_lambda_rejected`) and a marshalled parameter with no `lower` adapter (`conversion_test`, WI-088). Both still emit a compiling zero.\n\nTHE FIX DIRECTION, small: make the degraded body FAIL THE C++ BUILD while keeping the diagnostic at the exact method — `static_assert(false, \"<message>\")` is ill-formed in a non-template function and reports the message verbatim, so the author gets the codegen-time comment AND cannot ship it. Alternatives worth a moment: `#error` (kills the whole translation unit, losing the per-method locality), `throw std::logic_error(...)` (compiles and defers to run time — the same defect one level down), or promoting every degrade to a hard `CppCodegenError` (loses the 'emit the rest of the header' property the degrade exists for, and would need the two existing tests re-pointed).\n\nWATCH: a method emitted inside a TEMPLATE struct is a different case — `static_assert(false, ...)` in an uninstantiated template is ill-formed-no-diagnostic-required before C++23 and some compilers fire it eagerly; the dependent-false idiom (`static_assert(dependent_false_v<T>, ...)`) is what works there, and `anthill_runtime.hpp` is the natural home for `dependent_false_v`. Check both shapes — cpp-gen emits template traits classes routinely.\n\nACCEPTANCE: a body cpp-gen cannot lower produces output that DOES NOT COMPILE, carrying the codegen-time message; the two existing degrade tests (`unsupported_test::recursive_anonymous_lambda_rejected`, `conversion_test`'s missing-`lower` case) are updated to assert that and still see their diagnostics; the template-struct shape is covered by a driven test, not by assumption; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: found while delivering WI-886."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-31T13:09:50Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-891", author: "user", content: "WIDEN THE FIX: the DEFAULT is what is wrong, not just the payload. WI-886's /simplify review looked at what `synthesise_body_for` actually swallows and found that most of it is not a cpp-gen capability gap at all — these arms reach the degrade today and every one becomes `// TODO … return {};`:\n  * 'DeBruijn var in expression body — should have been opened by the loader'\n  * 'logic variable ?x appeared in an expression body'\n  * 'unresolved method call .foo reached cpp-gen'\n  * 'constructor X is neither a known entity nor a recognised collection literal'\nThose are program / invariant faults, and degrading them produces a header that compiles and answers zero. Only three arms are honestly 'not emitted yet' (HoApply / post-elaboration, `Expr::Spliced`, `Bottom`).\n\nSO THE SHAPE TO CONSIDER IS AN INVERSION: FATAL by default, degradation OPT-IN at the producers that mean it — rather than 'degrade everything, and make the payload non-compiling'. The two are not exclusive (a static_assert payload is still right for the opt-in arms), but the inversion is the one that stops the next unclassified error from silently returning zero.\n\nMECHANISM NOTE, measured against the alternative WI-886 rejected: making `lower_node` return a two-variant error is LOSSY as a drop-in. Its recursive helpers (`lower_let_chain_node`, `lower_match_branches_node`, `lower_stdlib_wrapper_node`, `lower_constructor_literal_node`) return `CppCodegenError` and re-enter `lower_node` with `?`, so a classification raised inside a `let` or a `match` arm is silently downgraded at that conversion unless every one of them changes too. WI-886 used a `Cell` on `CodegenContext` (`unrealized_refusal`, clear-then-read by the single degrading caller) precisely because it cannot lose the classification; an inversion that keeps a typed error must convert all the recursive helpers in the same commit, or it reintroduces the silent-downgrade one level down.\n\nALSO: WI-886 added the FIRST caller that must not degrade, so the `Cell`'s clear-then-read contract now has one reader and would need re-stating if a second degrading caller appears (a per-operation 'keep going' mode in project codegen is the obvious one). Whatever this ticket lands should subsume that flag rather than sit beside it.", at: "2026-07-31T05:49:31Z")
